{{main|2012 Indiana gubernatorial election}}
[[Governor of Indiana|Governor]] [[Mitch Daniels]] was term-limited in 2012.

[[Mike Pence]], a six-term Republican in the U.S. House of Representatives, announced his candidacy for his party's nomination. Pence, whose announcement was anticipated by his resignation of a leading position in the GOP caucus in the House, was regarded as the favorite for election.<ref name="Pence announcement">{{cite news|last=Muskal|first=Michael|title=Mike Pence to run for Indiana governor|url=https://www.latimes.com/news/politics/la-pn-pence-governor-20110506,0,3385004.story|access-date=May 5, 2011|newspaper=The Los Angeles Times|date=May 5, 2011}}</ref> Indianapolis businessman and former Hamilton County Councilman Jim Wallace had announced his candidacy for the Republican nomination prior to Pence's entrance but failed to collect enough signatures to become an official candidate by the deadline in February 2012.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.indystar.com/article/20120416/NEWS05/120416029/Pence-leads-rivals-fundraising-governor-s-race?odyssey=tab%7Ctopnews%7Ctext%7CNews|title=Pence leads rivals in fundraising for governor's race|work=[[Indianapolis Star]]|first=Mary Beth|last=Schneider|date=April 16, 2012}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.nationalreview.com/campaign-spot/258731/republican-announces-bid-indiana-governor|title=The Campaign Spot|work=[[National Review]]|first=Jim|last=Geraghty|date=February 2, 2011}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.indystar.com/article/20110202/NEWS05/110202007/Businessman-exploring-run-GOP-gubernatorial-nomination-2012|title=Businessman Exploring Run For GOP Gubernatorial Nomination|work=The Indianapolis Star|last=Schneider|first=Mary Beth|date=February 2, 2011}}</ref>

Former state House Speaker [[John R. Gregg]] was unopposed for the Democratic Party's nomination.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.politics1.com/in.htm|title=Politics1 - Online Guide to Indiana Elections, Candidates & Politics|first=Ron|last=Gunzburger|website=www.politics1.com}}</ref>

Former ''Survivor'' contestant and founder of the [[Rupert Boneham#Rupert's Kids|Rupert's Kids]] charity [[Rupert Boneham]] ran as the [[Libertarian Party (United States)|Libertarian Party]] candidate.<ref>{{Cite web|url=http://www.indystar.com/article/20120404/NEWS08/204040309/Matthew-Tully-Rupert-Boneham-plans-win-governor-s-seat|title=Matthew Tully &#124; Indianapolis Star &#124; indystar.com<!-- Bot generated title -->}}</ref>

Pence narrowly defeated Gregg with 49.9% of the vote to Gregg's 46.56%. Boneham received 3.95% of the vote.<ref>{{cite web | url=http://www.in.gov/apps/sos/election/general/general2012?page=office&countyID=-1&officeID=37&districtID=-1&candidate= | title =Election Results | publisher =in.gov/apps/sos | date =November 28, 2012 | access-date =January 19, 2014 }}</ref>

