{{main|2012 Puerto Rico gubernatorial election}}
[[Governor of Puerto Rico|Governor]] [[Luis Fortuño]] ([[New Progressive Party of Puerto Rico|PNP]]/R) ran for re-election.

Fortuño had been mentioned as a long-shot potential Republican nominee for president or vice president [[2012 United States presidential election|in 2012]].<ref>{{cite web|url=http://blog.newsweek.com/blogs/thegaggle/archive/2009/11/25/absurdly-premature-2012-watch-vol-2-the-governor-of-puerto-rico-for-president.aspx|title=The Governor of Puerto Rico ... for President?|date=November 26, 2009|access-date=November 26, 2009|work=Newsweek|first=Andrew|last=Romano|author-link=Andrew Romano|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20091127085950/http://blog.newsweek.com/blogs/thegaggle/archive/2009/11/25/absurdly-premature-2012-watch-vol-2-the-governor-of-puerto-rico-for-president.aspx|archive-date=November 27, 2009|url-status=dead|df=mdy-all}}</ref> However, he announced on June 26, 2011, that he would run for re-election instead of seeking the presidency.

[[Senate of Puerto Rico|Senator]] [[Alejandro García Padilla]] ([[Popular Democratic Party of Puerto Rico|PPD]]) was the net biggest challenger out of six challengers, and won the general election.

[[Puerto Rico]] does not have a position of lieutenant governor.

